        <table class="w-full text-sm border border-neutral-200">
          <thead><tr class="bg-neutral-100"><th class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2 text-left">Draw Type</th><th class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2 text-left">Typical CRS Range</th><th class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2 text-left">Invitations per Draw</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">General (all-program)</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">430 - 530</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">1,000 - 6,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">Healthcare</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">430 - 480</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">500 - 3,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">STEM</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">440 - 500</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">500 - 2,500</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">French language</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">380 - 430</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">500 - 3,500</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">Trades</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">430 - 480</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">500 - 1,500</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">Transport</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">430 - 470</td><td class="border border-neutral-200 px-4 py-2">500 - 1,000</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p><em>Note: The ranges above are approximate and based on historical data. Actual cutoffs vary with each draw.</em></p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-10 mb-4">What Happens After You Receive an ITA</h2>

      <p>Receiving an Invitation to Apply is a significant milestone, but it is not the end of the process. After receiving an ITA, you have exactly 60 calendar days to submit a complete application for permanent residence through your IRCC online account. This is a strict deadline - if you miss it, your ITA expires and you return to the Express Entry pool (though you can re-enter later).</p>

      <p>Your application must include a comprehensive set of supporting documents:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Valid passport or travel document</li>
        <li>Language test results (IELTS, CELPIP, TEF, or TCF, less than two years old)</li>
        <li>Educational Credential Assessment (ECA) report for foreign credentials</li>
        <li>Proof of Canadian and/or foreign work experience (reference letters, pay stubs, tax records)</li>
        <li>Police clearance certificates from every country where you lived six or more months since age 18</li>
        <li>Immigration medical examination results from a designated panel physician</li>
        <li>Proof of settlement funds (bank statements, investment records, or similar) unless exempt</li>
        <li>Digital photographs meeting IRCC specifications</li>
        <li>Provincial nomination certificate (if applicable)</li>
        <li>Job offer documentation and LMIA (if applicable)</li>
      </ul>

      <p>It is strongly advisable to begin gathering these documents before you receive an ITA. Police certificates from some countries can take weeks or months to obtain, and medical exam appointments may have limited availability. Having documents ready in advance ensures you can submit a complete application well within the 60-day window.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-10 mb-4">Strategies to Maximize Your Draw Chances</h2>

      <p>Beyond simply improving your CRS score, there are strategic approaches to maximizing your chances in any given draw. First, enter the Express Entry pool as early as possible. The tiebreaking mechanism favors earlier submissions, so even if you are still optimizing your score, having a profile in the pool gives you a time advantage. Second, keep your profile updated. If you retake a language test and achieve a higher score, or if you gain additional work experience, update your profile immediately so your CRS score reflects your current qualifications.</p>

      <p>Third, monitor draw trends closely. If category-based draws in your occupation have been occurring regularly, ensure your NOC code is correctly entered and that all relevant work experience is documented. Fourth, consider applying to Provincial Nominee Programs simultaneously, as a provincial nomination adds 600 CRS points and virtually guarantees an invitation. Many PNPs allow you to apply even while you are in the Express Entry pool.</p>

      <p>Finally, do not be discouraged by a single missed draw. The Express Entry system is dynamic, and cutoff scores fluctuate. A score that misses one draw by a few points may be well above the cutoff in the very next round. Persistence and continuous improvement are key to success in the Express Entry system.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-10 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4 not-prose">
        <details class="bg-white border border-neutral-200 rounded-lg p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-neutral-800 cursor-pointer">How often does Canada hold Express Entry draws?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">IRCC typically holds Express Entry draws every two weeks, though the frequency can vary. In some periods, draws have been held weekly. Category-based draws and general draws may occur in the same round or on separate dates. IRCC publishes all draw results on its official Rounds of Invitations page.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-neutral-200 rounded-lg p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-neutral-800 cursor-pointer">What are category-based Express Entry draws?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Category-based draws were introduced in 2023 and target candidates with specific qualifications, such as healthcare experience, STEM skills, trades experience, transport sector experience, agriculture and agri-food experience, or French language proficiency. These draws can have lower CRS cutoffs than general draws because they prioritize Canada's specific labor market needs.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-neutral-200 rounded-lg p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-neutral-800 cursor-pointer">What happens after I receive an Invitation to Apply (ITA)?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">After receiving an ITA, you have 60 calendar days to submit a complete application for permanent residence through your IRCC online account. Your application must include all supporting documents, such as police certificates, medical exams, proof of funds, language test results, Educational Credential Assessments, and reference letters from employers. Missing the deadline means the ITA expires.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

